      "id": 1,
      "text": "Tulelake takes its name from the nearby Tule Lake, a shallow body of water that once marked the edge of the Modoc War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulelake%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city officially became incorporated in 1937, after a land auction in 1931 sparked early settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulelake%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "World War II saw two camps near Tulelake: Camp Tulelake for German and Italian POWs, and the larger Tule Lake War Relocation Center for Japanese Americans.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulelake%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The western side of the former Tule Lake camp is now the townsite of Newell, a quiet community that grew from the camp’s grounds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
